      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ec17a5fd-404a-54aa-b298-fd7d944237c5",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{2}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=x^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (x^{2})-(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (x^{2})^{\\prime}}{(x^{2})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^4}\\\\ &=\\frac{2x(x\\cos x-2\\sin x)}{x^4}\\\\ &=\\frac{2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
